+# Live compatibility research record
+
+These live sources informed the architecture; the local snapshot remains the
+reproducible implementation authority.
+
+| Source title | URL | Access date | Decision informed |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Broadcom KB 430825, “VCF Upgrade Failure: Cannot Upgrade VCF 5.2.2 to 9.0.1 or 9.0.2 Due to NSX Back-in-Time Error (NSX 4.2.3.3)” | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/430825 | 2026-08-14 | Confirmed that NSX 4.2.3.3 build 25171318 cannot be upgraded in place to the older-dated 9.0.2 bundle build, and that the immediate-target path is unprepare, remove the domain, and re-import with a compatible NSX version. |
+| Broadcom KB 429205, “Overriding version of NSX Manager while converging or importing an existing vCenter instance with new NSX Manager deployment” | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/429205 | 2026-08-14 | Confirmed the custom-NSX import mechanism and the published NSX 4.2.3.1.0-24954727 / vCenter 8.0.3.00600-24853646 compatibility example used for the fresh intermediate deployment. |
+| Broadcom KB 390634, “Update Sequence for VCF 9.0 and Compatible VMware Products” | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/390634/update-sequence-for-vcf-90-and-compatibl.html | 2026-08-14 | Confirmed the core component order: SDDC Manager, NSX, vCenter, then ESXi. |
+| Broadcom KB 314608, “Correlating VMware Cloud Foundation version with the versions of its constituent products” | https://knowledge.broadcom.com/external/article/314608 | 2026-08-14 | Cross-checked the VCF 5.2.2 source BOM component versions and the target-release component family. |
+| Broadcom Product Interoperability Matrix | https://interopmatrix.broadcom.com/Interoperability | 2026-08-14 | Cross-checked component interoperability separately from whether a particular source-to-target transition is supported. |
+| Broadcom Upgrade Path matrix for VMware NSX | https://interopmatrix.broadcom.com/Upgrade?productId=912 | 2026-08-14 | Cross-checked the replacement NSX upgrade path instead of encoding the blocked direct edge. |
+
+The plan intentionally does not model 4.2.3.3 → 9.0.2 as an upgrade. Removing
+the newer NSX instance ends that lineage; the compatible 4.2.3.1 instance is a
+fresh deployment during re-import and has its own supported forward edge.
